{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01G5w3cyj4U19vbPJyjXz6pd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the research context carefully to define three tiers for the Dream simulation.\n\nKey facts from the data:\n- Function-level classification (mvp-b-train): F1 pass=87.1, fail=87.9, accuracy=87.5%\n- CWM baseline (generation): F1 pass=87.31, fail=87.71, accuracy=87.51%\n- These are essentially identical → confirmed null result at function level\n- Claim 1 now hinges on repo-level complexity-dependent advantage\n- Oracle sim shows per-test advantage depends heavily on T distribution and correlation structure\n\n\n- Real SWE-bench data shows 68.7% single-test cases with median T=1, so the advantage heavily depends on regression rates: at 5% regression the approach fails, but at 10-20% it succeeds\n- The critical unknowns are whether classification actually outperforms generation at repo level on SWE-bench and whether the function-level null result holds across multiple seeds\n- Other key questions: whether chain-of-thought helps classification, if diagnostic re-repair actually improves outcomes, and whether block correlation is empirically validated\n\nFor the best case, repo-level classification beats generation, diagnostic re-repair shows real gains, and block correlation checks out empirically—the full story works. In the median case, repo-level results are mixed or modest, diagnostic signals are weak, and block correlation is only partially validated, so I'd need to reframe around characterization rather than strong claims. Worst case is if repo-level also shows no advantage and diagnostics don't help, which would force a pivot toward an analysis paper about when per-test information matters.\n\nNow I'm setting concrete thresholds for each claim: repo-level classification needs at least a 5 percentage point F1 advantage for best case, 1-4 points for median, and less than 1 point is worst case. For diagnostic re-repair, I'm looking for at least 5% absolute improvement in resolve rate with per-test info as best case, 1-4% as median, and no significant improvement as worst. The oracle characterization is mostly already done, so I'm just checking whether the empirical block correlation validates the theoretical story.\n\nFor the function-level multi-seed analysis, I want to confirm the null hypothesis with tight confidence intervals as the ideal outcome, though some variance is acceptable, and the worst case would be if one formulation actually outperforms at function level, which would complicate the narrative. I'm now structuring the YAML with specific metrics to track: the F1 delta between classification and CWM at repo level, CWM's unparseable rate, the resolve rate improvement from diagnostic information, the empirical correlation of errors within instances, and the standard deviation of F1 across multiple seeds at function level.\n\nFor the trace-then-judge metric, I'm estimating that function-level F1 sits around 87.5% for both approaches. At the repo level where code complexity is much higher, I'm working through plausible performance ranges—baseline classification might hit 60-80% F1 while CWM struggles more with longer outputs. My best-case scenario has classification at 72-78% F1 versus CWM at 60-68%, yielding an 8-14 percentage point delta, whereas the median case narrows that to 2-6 points, and the worst case shows minimal separation below 2 points.\n\nReconsidering the repo-level CWM performance, I realize token budget exhaustion could cause catastrophic failure with high unparseable rates, or it could perform reasonably well if test outputs stay short. So I'm revising my estimates: best case shows classification F1 of 70-78% against CWM's 55-65% with 15-30% unparseable outputs and a 10-18 point delta, plus 4-8% improvement from re-repair attempts and block correlation around 0.3-0.6. The median scenario is tighter—classification at 65-72%, CWM at 60-68% with lower unparseable rates of 5-15%, delta of 3-8 points, and more modest re-repair gains of 1-3%.\n\nNow I need to verify the worst case maintains proper ordering and check whether the function-level null result holds cleanly across multiple seeds, since that affects all tiers. The trace-then-judge comparison is also critical—if it matches direct classification, that supports the idea that task formulation matters more than reasoning; if it outperforms, that suggests chain-of-thought helps; if it underperforms, the simpler approach wins.\n\nGiven the May 25 deadline with only 14 days remaining, I should define realistic tier thresholds. Best case means the complexity story fully validates with at least 8pp F1 gap between classifier and CWM at repo level, high unparseable rates for CWM (15%+), meaningful re-repair gains (3%+), and clean function-level nulls across seeds. Median case would show partial complexity signals but require pivoting toward a mixed narrative.\n\nFor the worst case, if repo-level experiments show negligible differences (under 2pp) or can't finish in time, I'd drop the complexity-dependent advantage claim entirely and reframe the paper around efficiency gains and oracle characterization of when per-test matters, with function-level equivalence as supporting evidence.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the execution risks—the swebench-pertest-data task alone needs 16-24 hours of Docker work, repo-level training requires 12 GPU hours plus infrastructure setup, and with 14 days total and various dependencies, there's real risk of not completing everything. I should factor this into my contingency planning.\n\nWriting the final YAML...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10251,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10251},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a3d1451c-b7b3-4cbc-a071-708995f197b9","uuid":"b0a693f8-6da5-4bc6-b56f-a93f5bb02c80"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01G5w3cyj4U19vbPJyjXz6pd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"Complexity-dependent advantage validated — repo-level gap large enough to carry the paper\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Repo-level clf F1 显著高于 CWM（≥8pp），CWM 在长输出上 token 预算耗尽导致 unparseable rate 高\"\n      - \"Multi-seed 确认 function-level null（F1 std < 0.5pp，paired bootstrap p > 0.3）\"\n      - \"Trace-then-judge ≤ direct classification（支持 task formulation > explicit reasoning 叙事）\"\n      - \"Diagnostic re-repair 在 multi-F2P 子集上 resolve rate 提升 ≥3%\"\n      - \"Empirical block correlation ρ ≥ 0.3，验证 oracle 分析的 block 假设\"\n    number_ranges:\n      func_level_clf_f1: [86.5, 88.0]\n      func_level_cwm_f1: [86.5, 88.0]\n      func_level_delta_pp: [-0.5, 0.5]\n      func_level_f1_std_across_seeds: [0.1, 0.5]\n      repo_level_clf_f1: [70, 78]\n      repo_level_cwm_f1: [55, 65]\n      repo_level_delta_pp: [8, 18]\n      cwm_unparseable_rate_repo_pct: [15, 35]\n      trace_then_judge_vs_clf_delta_pp: [-2, 0]\n      rerepair_resolve_rate_delta_pct: [3, 8]\n      empirical_block_corr_rho: [0.3, 0.6]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      原始 framing 完整成立。标题级 claim：classification 和 generation 在 function-level 等价，\n      但 repo-level 出现 8-18pp 差距，因为 CWM 的 token-budget exhaustion 和 string-value\n      prediction errors 在复杂输出上触发。三个 claims 全部保留：(1) complexity-dependent advantage\n      作为主贡献，(2) diagnostic re-repair 作为实用验证，(3) oracle frontier 作为部署决策框架。\n      Paper 定位：method paper with empirical validation。投 EMNLP main 有竞争力。\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"Repo-level 有信号但不够强，需要重心移向 characterization\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Repo-level clf F1 高于 CWM 但差距中等（2-7pp），或优势仅集中在 multi-F2P 子集\"\n      - \"CWM unparseable rate 中等（5-15%），部分可通过 truncation heuristic 修复\"\n      - \"Multi-seed 确认 function-level null\"\n      - \"Diagnostic signal 可测量（per-test precision/recall 合理），但 re-repair 改进 marginal（1-2%）\"\n      - \"Block correlation 部分验证（ρ = 0.1-0.3），oracle 预测定性成立但定量偏差\"\n    number_ranges:\n      func_level_clf_f1: [86.5, 88.0]\n      func_level_cwm_f1: [86.5, 88.0]\n      func_level_delta_pp: [-0.5, 0.5]\n      func_level_f1_std_across_seeds: [0.1, 0.8]\n      repo_level_clf_f1: [63, 72]\n      repo_level_cwm_f1: [58, 68]\n      repo_level_delta_pp: [2, 7]\n      cwm_unparseable_rate_repo_pct: [5, 15]\n      trace_then_judge_vs_clf_delta_pp: [-1, 2]\n      rerepair_resolve_rate_delta_pct: [0.5, 2.5]\n      empirical_block_corr_rho: [0.1, 0.3]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Claim 1 降级为 \"trend\"：classification 在 repo-level 有优势趋势但不 dramatic，强调\n      efficiency-equivalent accuracy（同等 F1，1-token vs N-token inference）作为补充论点。\n      Claim 2 降为 hypothesis + preliminary evidence，不作为独立贡献。Claim 3 oracle\n      characterization 升级为主贡献——论文重心从 \"method X beats method Y\" 转向\n      \"when and why does per-test verification help\"。标题改为强调 characterization：\n      \"When Does Per-Test Execution Simulation Help Code Repair?\"。Paper 定位：empirical\n      analysis paper with methodological insight。EMNLP Findings 合理，ICSE 2027 作为备选\n      更合适（SE venue 更接受 characterization 贡献）。\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"Repo-level 也是 null（或实验未完成），complexity story 不成立\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Repo-level clf ≈ CWM（delta < 2pp），或 SWE-bench Docker 基础设施问题导致实验未在 deadline 前完成\"\n      - \"CWM 在 repo-level 的 unparseable rate 低（<5%），因为 SWE-bench 测试输出实际较短\"\n      - \"Diagnostic re-repair 无显著改进（< 1%），per-test 信号噪声过大无法 actionable\"\n      - \"Block correlation 弱（ρ < 0.1），实际预测误差接近 IID——oracle 分析中 block 模型 overstates advantage\"\n      - \"Trace-then-judge 意外优于 direct classification（+2-4pp），削弱 'simpler formulation is better' 叙事\"\n    number_ranges:\n      func_level_clf_f1: [86.5, 88.0]\n      func_level_cwm_f1: [86.5, 88.0]\n      func_level_delta_pp: [-0.5, 0.5]\n      func_level_f1_std_across_seeds: [0.1, 1.2]\n      repo_level_clf_f1: [58, 66]\n      repo_level_cwm_f1: [57, 66]\n      repo_level_delta_pp: [-1, 2]\n      cwm_unparseable_rate_repo_pct: [0, 5]\n      trace_then_judge_vs_clf_delta_pp: [0, 4]\n      rerepair_resolve_rate_delta_pct: [-0.5, 1.0]\n      empirical_block_corr_rho: [0.0, 0.1]\n    narrative_direction: >\n      Complexity-dependent advantage claim 放弃。Paper 重构为三层：(1) Equivalence finding——\n      classification 和 generation 在 function-level 和 repo-level 均等价，这本身是关于 LLM\n      code understanding 的 non-trivial 发现（模型在 1-token 约束下保留了等价判断能力），\n      framing 为 \"efficiency-equivalent verification\"（同等准确率，推理成本降低 O(output_len)）；\n      (2) Oracle characterization 作为主贡献——per-test advantage 的理论条件和 SWE-bench\n      实际分布分析，诚实报告 T=1 主导（68.7%）限制了实际收益；(3) SWE-bench-PerTest 数据集\n      作为 resource contribution（如果完成）。Paper 定位从 method paper 变为 analysis paper：\n      \"Classification vs Generation for Code Execution Simulation: An Empirical Equivalence\n      and When Per-Test Granularity Matters\"。目标 EMNLP Findings 或 ICSE 2027。需要额外的\n      negative-result framing 功夫——强调 \"knowing when something doesn't help is as valuable\n      as showing it does\"。\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10251,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10251},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a3d1451c-b7b3-4cbc-a071-708995f197b9","uuid":"2c20d608-5b1f-4c56-9936-39353cf9ad92"}
